# The Mauryan Empire: India's First Pan-Subcontinental State

> Between 322 and 185 BCE the Mauryas welded nearly the whole subcontinent
> into one state — from Afghanistan to Karnataka. Its monument is not a
> palace but a set of rock and pillar edicts in which Ashoka, shaken by the
> Kalinga war, preached Dhamma to the entire population.

## 1. Sources for the Mauryas

| Source | Author / Nature | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Arthashastra | Kautilya (Chanakya) | Treatise on statecraft: spies, taxes, army |
| Indica | Megasthenes, Seleucus's ambassador | Eyewitness account (survives in fragments) |
| Mudrarakshasa | Vishakhadatta (play) | Chandragupta-Chanakya vs the Nandas |
| Ashokan edicts | Royal proclamations | Contemporary, public, dated |

The **Arthashastra**, a 15-book manual recovered and published by R.
Shamasastry in 1909, details the machinery of an intrusive state.
**Megasthenes**, envoy of **Seleucus Nicator** to Chandragupta's
Pataliputra, described a city of wood guarded by 570 towers. The edicts,
cut mainly in **Brahmi** (Kharosthi in the north-west), were finally read
in **1837** by **James Prinsep** — the discovery that made Ashoka
historical.

## 2. Chandragupta Maurya (322-298 BCE)

Guided by **Kautilya**, Chandragupta overthrew the unpopular Nanda king of
Magadha and took Pataliputra by about **322 BCE**. His war with **Seleucus
Nicator** (c. 305 BCE) ended in a treaty ceding the eastern satrapies —
Arachosia (Kandahar), Gedrosia (Makran), Paropamisadae (Kabul) — in
exchange for **500 war elephants** and a marriage alliance, with
Megasthenes left as resident ambassador.

[important title="An emperor's Jain exit"]
Jain tradition says Chandragupta, in old age, abdicated, went south with
the sage Bhadrabahu to **Shravanabelagola** (Karnataka), and ended his life
by ritual fast — **sallekhana** — about 298 BCE.
[/important]

## 3. Bindusara (298-273 BCE)

Called **Amitraghata** ("destroyer of foes"; Greek *Amitrochates*),
Bindusara consolidated the Deccan — only Kalinga remained outside — kept
the Seleucid connection (**Deimachus** was Antiochus I's envoy), and
patronised the **Ajivika** sect.

## 4. Ashoka the Great (268-232 BCE)

Ashoka's personal name was discovered only in 1915 at **Maski** (and later
Gujjara), where a minor edict reads "Devanampiya **Asoka**"; elsewhere he
calls himself Devanampiya Piyadasi.

- **Kalinga war, 261 BCE:** his own 13th Rock Edict records 100,000 slain,
  150,000 deported — and his turn to Dhamma in remorse.
- **Dhamma** was an ethical code, not mere Buddhism: respect for elders
  and slaves, generosity, tolerance of all sects, propagated by officers
  called **dhamma mahamatras**.
- Rock Edict XIII names five Greek kings — **Antiochus II** of Syria,
  **Ptolemy II** of Egypt, **Antigonus** of Macedonia, **Magas** of Cyrene,
  **Alexander** of Epirus — proving diplomatic contact across the
  Hellenistic world.
- He convened the **Third Buddhist Council** at Pataliputra (250 BCE) and
  sent his children **Mahendra and Sanghamitra** as missionaries to **Sri
  Lanka**; the Barabar caves he gave to the Ajivikas.

## 5. The Edicts: The First Mass Media

| Type | Number | Famous sites |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Major Rock Edicts | 14 | Girnar, Kalsi, Shahbazgarhi, Erragudi |
| Minor Rock Edicts | several | Maski, Gujjara, Rupnath, Sasaram |
| Pillar Edicts | 7 | Delhi-Topra, Rampurva, Lauriya Nandangarh |
| Separate (Kalinga) Edicts | 2 | Dhauli, Jaugada |

The **Kandahar** bilingual (Greek-Aramaic) edict shows the empire speaking
to subjects in their own scripts; **Sarnath's** four-lion capital, in
polished Chunar sandstone, became India's national emblem in 1950.

## 6. Mauryan Administration

@diagram tree id="mauryan-rulers" layout="tree" aria="The Mauryan ruling line from Chandragupta to Brihadratha"
  Mauryas -> "Chandragupta (322-298 BCE)"
  Mauryas -> "Bindusara (298-273 BCE)"
  Mauryas -> "Ashoka (268-232 BCE)"
  Mauryas -> "Later Mauryas"
  Later Mauryas -> "Dasharatha, Samprati, Salisuka"
  Later Mauryas -> "Brihadratha (killed 185 BCE)"
@/diagram

- **Centre:** king advised by the **mantriparishad**; the top 18 posts,
  the **tirthas**, included the revenue chief (samaharta) and treasurer
  (sannidhata).
- **Provinces:** four, each under a prince — **Uttarapatha** (Taxila),
  **Avantiratha** (Ujjain), **Dakshinapatha** (Suvarnagiri), and **Kalinga**
  (Tosali).
- **Intelligence:** **gudhapurushas** — spies stationary (*sanstha*) and
  wandering (*sanchara*) — the Arthashastra's nervous heart.
- **City:** Megasthenes reports six committees of five members each at
  Pataliputra, handling trade, foreigners, and the tithe on sales.
- **Land:** the king's own domain, **Sit** land, sat beside taxed private
  plots at roughly one-sixth of produce.

