## 7. Economy and Society

Punch-marked silver coins (**pana**) circulated under state regulation of
weights; the crown ran mines, forests, and armouries. Ashoka's Pillar
Edict VII records wells, rest houses, and medical plantations for men and
cattle — the first recorded welfare policy in India.

[definition]
**Sit land** — the crown's own agricultural land, farmed with slave and
hired labour under state supervision, its produce flowing straight into
the Mauryan treasury.
[/definition]

## 8. Decline (185 BCE)

Ashoka died in 232 BCE; weak successors followed. In **185 BCE** the
commander-in-chief **Pushyamitra Shunga** killed the last king,
Brihadratha, during a troop inspection and founded the Shunga dynasty.

| Theory | Author | Claim |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Brahmanical reaction | H. P. Shastri | Anti-sacrifice policy bred priestly revolt |
| Financial crisis | D. D. Kosambi | Debased coins point to fiscal breakdown |
| Over-centralisation | Romila Thapar | No single cause: too little local agency |

[note]
Modern historians do not blame Ashoka's pacifism: the empire outlived him
by fifty years, and Pushyamitra's coup was a court affair, not a popular
rising. Mauryan pillars outlasted the dynasty by centuries.
[/note]

## Key Takeaways

- The Mauryas (322-185 BCE) created the subcontinent's first uniform
  administration, documented in the Arthashastra and Megasthenes's Indica.
- Ashoka's edicts — read after Prinsep's 1837 decipherment of Brahmi — are
  India's first dated public policy statements.
- Kalinga (261 BCE) turned Ashoka from conqueror to Dhamma's preacher;
  his missions carried Buddhism abroad.
- Four provinces, the 18 tirthas, gudhapurasha spies, and Sit land formed
  the working machinery — and its heavy cost hastened the fall.
